WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.320
One-off set bad?

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.720
The last time we had a “block” of two or&nbsp;
more consecutive sets on the same plane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.000
was about three years ago, with every set&nbsp;
since being a one-off set. Not only this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.560
but there hasn’t been a multi-set block in a&nbsp;
brand new plane since Ixalan block concluded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.440
more than eight years ago. These facts stand&nbsp;
in stark contrast to earlier eras of Magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.360
which for decades had us venture to one&nbsp;
plane a year over the course of three sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.400
often a brand new plane. To be clear, this change&nbsp;
was intentional, its communication was public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.520
and its reasons for happening were on some level&nbsp;
understandable. Later sets in blocks sold worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.400
than earlier ones, limited environments were&nbsp;
tricky to make satisfying, and a set with lukewarm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.280
reception getting followed up with an immediate&nbsp;
double and triple-down to the same setting was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.480
certainly not an ideal outcome for developers&nbsp;
or players, you might reasonably say. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.680
to lay my cards on the table here, I do not like&nbsp;
the new status quo we find ourselves in. Having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.200
every set be a new plane, particularly with five&nbsp;
or more sets per year, makes things feel rushed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.600
makes it feel like there’s no time to breathe&nbsp;
and to become immersed in a particular world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.320
And, if I really wanted to let nostalgia take&nbsp;
the wheel on this one, I could tell you that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.600
gradual pivot from three set blocks to purely&nbsp;
one-off sets has been an unambiguous tragedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.680
that we as magic players are fallen and need to&nbsp;
ret-vrn the glory days of 2013, where Magic sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.560
came in threes and where bold duels could take&nbsp;
place on a middle school lunch table. I’d love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.800
to be able to contribute yet another uncomplicated&nbsp;
piece of ammunition to the siege on the decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.600
currently being made by Hasbro, but in truth the&nbsp;
issue of blocks is a bit more complicated than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.400
that. I do think there is something wrong with&nbsp;
what we’re currently doing, where standard-legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.080
sets take us to 6 or 7 different planes every&nbsp;
single year, but three set blocks don’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.520
represent a better vision of the game, just one&nbsp;
with a different set of problems. So here I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.320
to lay out my case for what has gone wrong, both&nbsp;
past and present, and talk about an alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.200
approach to the way we conceptualize set&nbsp;
structuring. And, I’ll be getting into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.880
very specific missed opportunity that occurred&nbsp;
in 2024 that provides a fascinating case study.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.560
To start with, here’s a very brief timeline of&nbsp;
blocks. The first conventional three-set block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.160
was Mirage block in 1996, and this would set a&nbsp;
trend of three-set blocks beginning in October&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.080
that would continue basically uninterrupted&nbsp;
for the following 18 years until the release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.760
of Battle for Zendikar. There were also yearly or&nbsp;
semi-yearly core sets over this span of time, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:31.440
aside from that and some extras like masters sets&nbsp;
and un-sets, the general idea was one block a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.120
and three sets per block. Then, in 2015, Magic:&nbsp;
the Gathering switched to two set blocks, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.640
this pattern would continue until War of the Spark&nbsp;
in 2019, which was either a standalone set or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.360
third set of the third Ravnica block, depending&nbsp;
on how you count. From there, sets were a mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.160
of one-offs like Kalheim and two set blocks that&nbsp;
returned to a pre-existing plane such as Midnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.760
Hunt, and the final two-set return-to-block ended&nbsp;
about three years ago with the release of March of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.560
the Machine. Ever since, it’s been all one-offs,&nbsp;
with consecutive sets never featuring the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.200
setting. And, I guess I’ll start my discussion&nbsp;
of blocks by saying that the current era of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.320
blocks we’re in does not feel very good, and on a&nbsp;
level pretty fundamental to the way we experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.360
the game. Obviously there are other factors&nbsp;
contributing to the way Magic feels in 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.480
but the set structure being all one-offs isn’t&nbsp;
insignificant. The current progression of things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.960
offers no time to stop and breathe in a particular&nbsp;
plane, no time to be interested about what it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.720
to offer next, and no spare moments to stop and&nbsp;
reflect. Storylines are bouncing and disconnected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.880
since we can never stop and watch how a&nbsp;
particular plane changes for any length of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.680
only get a fleeting snapshot of it before we’re&nbsp;
off to the next place. Thought and effort is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.040
put into how sets on different planes tie into one&nbsp;
another mechanically for standard purposes, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.920
don’t really get to see iterations on a mechanical&nbsp;
theme like we did sometime in the past—everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.780
a plane has to offer needs to be immediately&nbsp;
present since there’s only one set worth of it.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:56.800
Three-set block bad?

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.040
So as you can tell, I really don’t think what&nbsp;
we’re doing in the present is ideal. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.720
that doesn’t mean that the ways sets were&nbsp;
structured in the past were necessarily better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.640
and to explore that we really&nbsp;
need to talk about 3-set blocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.160
This was the way Magic sets were&nbsp;
organized for years and years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.760
and here I want to hone in on the three blocks I&nbsp;
was fully around for: Return to Ravnica, Theros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.200
and Khans of Tarkir. RTR was structured into the&nbsp;
namesake set that contained five of the guilds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.720
Gatecrash which featured the other five, and&nbsp;
Dragon’s Maze which featured all ten guilds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.520
That final set also focused on “The Maze” which&nbsp;
was a sort of structure built into the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.720
and which would host a race in which a “runner”&nbsp;
from each guild would travel to the “Maze’s End”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.280
and this process would ultimately help decide the&nbsp;
balance of power in Ravnica. This is all fine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.880
cool lore-wise, but it didn’t really get conveyed&nbsp;
that well in the Dragon’s Maze set. The set was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.200
small and thus felt kinda cramped with 10 guilds,&nbsp;
and the things explicitly mentioning parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:59.520
lore—the gates, the gatekeepers, and Maze’s End&nbsp;
itself—were both few and honestly pretty bad. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.800
didn’t even know the legendary creatures of&nbsp;
the set were supposed to be Maze-Runners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.520
because the set didn’t really draw attention to&nbsp;
this element of the story at all. Dragon’s Maze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.600
simply didn’t feel like a proper third set,&nbsp;
rather just a little bit of extra Ravnica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.720
with some attached plot that not enough effort&nbsp;
was put into guiding us through with its design

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.120
The following two blocks also each contained a&nbsp;
set that didn’t really fit in, but in the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.320
of those it was the middle set. Theros the set&nbsp;
established the plane as a setting based around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.040
Greek myths, and also introduced characters&nbsp;
such as Elspeth as a monster-slaying champion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.480
and Xenagos as a satyr planeswalker who aspired&nbsp;
to godhood. Born of the Gods was the second set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.960
of the block, and featured the story of Xenagos&nbsp;
ascending to godhood amidst growing chaos in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.280
pantheon. Journey into Nyx was the third&nbsp;
set, and focused on the consequences of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.640
Xenagos’s Godhood and the ensuing showdown that&nbsp;
resulted from it. That all sounds fine and good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.520
but in terms of gameplay, the second&nbsp;
set—Born of the Gods—was very strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.920
and a big reason why it stands out as odd is&nbsp;
that the other two sets actually did a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.680
great job conveying the progression&nbsp;
of the block through their mechanics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.080
Theros was more focused on the mortal plane&nbsp;
through its heavy usage of heroic and monstrosity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.640
as mechanics, whereas Journey into Nyx was much&nbsp;
more focused around the pantheon of gods and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.200
conflict within it, which could be seen in its&nbsp;
Constellation mechanic and extra-high density of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.320
enchantments. Born of the Gods, on the other hand,&nbsp;
featured a couple kinda weird mechanics and didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.400
really convey a strong sense of place or time. It&nbsp;
felt more like Theros B-sides than anything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.560
just some kinda odd designs to go along with&nbsp;
a group of five gods in order to allow the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:38.160
fifteen gods to be spread evenly between three&nbsp;
sets. It played an undeniable role in tracing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.280
Xenagos’s storyline, but in most ways other&nbsp;
than that the set was a bit of a disappointment.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.840
The same goes to a lesser extent for the&nbsp;
Tarkir Block—Khans of Tarkir focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.840
five warring factions in a world where&nbsp;
Dragons have gone extinct, and Dragons of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.160
Tarkir features an alternate timeline where&nbsp;
Dragons are still around and where Tarkir’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.280
clans and characters have altered fates. The&nbsp;
middle set, Fate Reforged, was a little odd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.680
though. Lore-wise it involves Sarkhan traveling&nbsp;
back in time to when Dragons were still around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.480
alongside the five clans, and intervening&nbsp;
in the battle between Ugin and Nicol Bolas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.680
This is cool as a way to transition between Khans&nbsp;
and Dragons, but in terms of gameplay it just sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.720
of felt like an awkward mashup of the two sets.&nbsp;
Fate Reforged used three clan keywords from Khans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.600
and two of the changed ones from DTK, it had&nbsp;
some dragons rather than none or a whole bunch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.440
and the clans were less-three-color&nbsp;
focused than Khans but not quite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.120
two-color pairings in Dragons. For a middle&nbsp;
set separated from the other two by 1300 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.080
this feels more like the middle of an animorphs&nbsp;
cover than it does its own cohesive world. To be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.000
clear, it is still easily the most defensible&nbsp;
third set of the three blocks I’ve mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.240
and to be honest I can imagine this feeling like&nbsp;
a genuinely compelling three set block with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.360
handful of changes to the middle set. Nonetheless,&nbsp;
in the form it ended up in, Fate Reforged feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.840
like a continuation of a trend—one of the sets&nbsp;
in a block being a little awkward, and feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.541
more like a stepping stone for story purposes&nbsp;
than a fully thought out landscape of its own.

00:08:02.541 --> 00:08:02.560
Two-set block good?

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.040
So, these are the two extremes. On the one hand,&nbsp;
you’ve got the current status quo where every set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.960
is a new plane and no setting ever has time&nbsp;
to breathe or experience change, and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.360
have the status quo of years past where three set&nbsp;
blocks are the norm and usually feature an awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.160
second or third set mostly there for storytelling&nbsp;
purposes. Naturally, I would prefer that we find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.320
our way back to a nice in-between, although&nbsp;
I will say that I don’t think that in-between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.280
would be an endless series of two-set blocks&nbsp;
like we had in the mid-to-late 2010s. One-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:34.080
sets can be great as a way to do something funky&nbsp;
and unusual, to explore some bold new aesthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.040
territory without committing too hard, and to have&nbsp;
a set where following a story isn’t necessarily a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.520
main priority. Edge of Eternities was a great&nbsp;
one-off set to shake the game up with some out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.600
there aesthetics and new mechanics, and I imagine&nbsp;
Wizards of the Coast is enjoying the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.640
to gauge community feedback on something like&nbsp;
this. The same goes for Duskmourn—I could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.800
taken two sets of that, but for the playerbase at&nbsp;
large it makes sense as an interesting experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.960
into an unusual setting with a more modern&nbsp;
aesthetic, and a way to introduce a cool new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.920
subtype like rooms that plays into that setting.&nbsp;
Even Outlaws at Thunder Junction would have felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.320
a lot better if it was surrounded by a couple&nbsp;
two set blocks. It’s okay to have a set that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:16.000
a little silly, a little gimmicky, and a little&nbsp;
odd story-wise—it’s okay to have a hat set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.120
However, in order for that sort of set to feel&nbsp;
justified, it kind of has to feel differentiated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.680
in some way from what surrounds it, has to have&nbsp;
a degree of adjacency to a non-hat set for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:31.040
sake of contrast, right? It might not be clear&nbsp;
exactly what I mean here, so now is a good time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.000
as any to talk about the specific appeals I&nbsp;
find in two set blocks, and why I think they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.800
should once again become a staple part of the&nbsp;
way we structure sets, alongside one-off sets.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.880
The biggest reason I like two-set blocks is that&nbsp;
they’re able to tell the story of a place much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.760
better than a one-set block could. Let’s jump back&nbsp;
to the three set blocks set on brand new planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.640
I mentioned earlier—Theros and Tarkir. Setting&nbsp;
aside the awkward second set in each of these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:59.680
we can notice a pattern here: set one establishes&nbsp;
a setting, and then a later set complicates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.560
setting, introducing new elements and showcasing&nbsp;
a dramatically changed version of it. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.200
wonderfully blunt form of storytelling, and seeing&nbsp;
that clear change communicated through the design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.760
of the set would be great for getting players to&nbsp;
engage more with the lore and ask more questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.320
as opposed to one-off sets that present a snapshot&nbsp;
of things and then dash away at full speed. Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:23.280
set blocks with a dramatic change between set&nbsp;
one and set two can also draw out the lifespan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.160
of a given setting without letting things feel&nbsp;
stagnant, and without causing the negative impacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.720
to limited that have been caused by a lot of&nbsp;
blocks in the past. If both sets of a given block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.200
are full-sized and contain their own important&nbsp;
features and distinguishing characteristics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.765
you’ll be getting the benefits of being able to&nbsp;
tell a story and letting players stay engaged with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.765 --> 00:10:43.120
how a particular plane is changing for a longer&nbsp;
span of time, but you’ll also get the benefits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.920
being able to present each set as something cool&nbsp;
and different, and you can even make each playable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.640
as its own standalone limited format if that’s&nbsp;
a concern. Now, all of this might still seem a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:58.320
little abstract, so how about we talk about some&nbsp;
specific examples, a few sets since 2023 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.720
might have been nice as two-set blocks, and which&nbsp;
to me kind of represent missed opportunities.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.920
Murders at Karlov Manor stands out as an&nbsp;
interesting case study, because it’s a set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.680
where they were clearly going for a version&nbsp;
of the establish-then-change framework I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:17.680
mentioned earlier. Ravnica Remastered came out in&nbsp;
January of 2024, followed by MKM a month later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.240
and you can kind of see the vision here—remind&nbsp;
people what Ravnica is, then do something cool and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:26.400
different with the Ravnica setting. I actually&nbsp;
think this framework could have worked here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.520
without that many changes to MKM itself—instead&nbsp;
of Ravnica Remastered one month in advance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:36.400
drop an original new Ravnica set two months in&nbsp;
advance. In that set, you’d want to establish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:41.040
the characters and setting that would be relevant&nbsp;
for the following set, and importantly, you’d want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:46.560
to feature the guilds! They could maybe be a bit&nbsp;
less present than previous Ravnica sets, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.920
definitely need to be there in the first place&nbsp;
and they need to have some sort of tie-in with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:55.600
the story of Murders at Karlov manor, because why&nbsp;
else are we returning to Ravnica again? There’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:01.120
shortage of places you could set a murder mystery,&nbsp;
and choosing Ravnica, the plane about guilds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:07.360
with only a feeble little “oh yeah, this plane&nbsp;
exists” reprints set a month before is just a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:13.920
odd choice. So, this is one example of a return-to&nbsp;
set that needed an accompanying set to work well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:17.360
and I also think Lorwyn Eclipsed would have&nbsp;
been another great candidate to be a two set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:23.600
block. It’s an iconic and beloved plane, and the&nbsp;
original Lorwyn super-block was broken into two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:29.920
separate two-set blocks, one for Lorwyn and one&nbsp;
for its darker counterpart, Shadowmoor. The Lorwyn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.360
Eclipsed set we got was far from bad, but it did&nbsp;
feel a bit rushed as a one-set return to a setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:41.440
that was originally built-out with four sets, and&nbsp;
I have to imagine a two set Lorwyn + Shadowmoor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:46.080
pairing would have made for an appealing choice.&nbsp;
So, there are clear instances where I think we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.960
could have benefitted from a two-set block&nbsp;
when returning to an iconic plane. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:57.440
to me the biggest missed opportunity of the past&nbsp;
three years was actually a new set: Bloomburrow.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:03.280
I truly cannot think of a more obvious slam dunk&nbsp;
of a two-set block than Bloomburrow. Any competent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.680
market research would have suggested this was&nbsp;
going to be a very popular and well-received set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.720
and the final product indicates that&nbsp;
Wizards of the Coast was willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:16.000
put in the necessary effort to make it great.&nbsp;
Not only this, but it self-justifies being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:20.560
two set block in beautifully simple fashion,&nbsp;
and we can see an indication of this in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:25.120
full art lands. Imagine this: the first&nbsp;
set of the block is set in late spring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:29.680
and this is where we’re introduced to this&nbsp;
vibrant world and all its creature types. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.640
the second set of the block takes place in autumn,&nbsp;
and there’s decidedly more urgency and darkness as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:40.320
the various creatures “prepare for winter” in some&nbsp;
sense. What exactly that looks like or how literal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:45.760
vs metaphorical the preparing for winter element&nbsp;
is could depend on a lot of things, but regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:50.960
it seems like a very natural two-part progression.&nbsp;
Create a setting, and then complicate it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:55.680
if we were still in an era where two-set blocks&nbsp;
are an accepted part of life, this all lines up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:00.320
perfectly—a plane that feels cohesive with the MTG&nbsp;
universe, an aesthetic that is almost guaranteed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:04.400
to be widely appealing, and a setting that very&nbsp;
naturally features change over time that can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:09.520
played with in interesting ways. But, we’re&nbsp;
not in that sort of era. We haven’t been in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:14.960
an era where a new plane could get its own block&nbsp;
for more than eight years. So, it didn’t happen.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:15.200
Synthesizing the sauce

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.440
I vividly remember the excitement of following&nbsp;
the sets in a block when I was in my first few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.480
years of playing the game. These were localized,&nbsp;
tangible stories about what was happening in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.040
particular place, rather than being just one&nbsp;
puzzle piece in a grand metanarrative about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.080
“The Omenpaths” or something. Anecdotally, when&nbsp;
I talk to people who started playing Magic more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:38.480
recently and ask what sets they were especially&nbsp;
engaged with as they were getting into the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:44.640
a frequent answer is the four set Phyrexia arc&nbsp;
that concluded in 2023. That observation may or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:49.200
may not represent a wide swathe of the playerbase,&nbsp;
but it’s not hard to imagine why people might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:53.680
excited about something like that: it’s a story&nbsp;
as central to the broader Magic lore as it gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:57.840
told over the span of four sets with time&nbsp;
for anticipation to build as the Phyrexian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:02.480
threat ramped up. If Bloomburrow had been&nbsp;
a two set block, I have to imagine we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:07.440
have seen something similar, possibly to an even&nbsp;
greater extent: large swathes of players fondly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.440
recounting their introduction into the game&nbsp;
being a months-long period where the Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.240
the Gathering found itself centered in a lushly&nbsp;
realized landscape straight out of Redwall.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:22.080
In a blog post last year, Mark Rosewater laid out&nbsp;
his mindset on blocks in general and Bloomburrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:27.200
in specific, and it’s really interesting as&nbsp;
a documentation of how a major decisionmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:32.400
conceptualizes money, people, and how the two&nbsp;
intersect. It starts with the objective metric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:37.520
that sales are almost always lower for subsequent&nbsp;
sets in a block, and then uses this data point to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:42.320
make a couple strange extrapolations from there:&nbsp;
that players “don’t want to stay in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:47.200
world for consecutive sets,” and that players&nbsp;
“respond better given a gap between visits” to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:53.920
a given plane. The issue is, those aren’t things&nbsp;
that sales data can tell you. There are a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:59.360
reasons why subsequent sets in a block might sell&nbsp;
worse, and imagining that a hypothetical second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:04.240
Bloomburrow set hypothetically selling worse than&nbsp;
the first would have equated to a players sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:09.040
around being kinda glum about returning&nbsp;
to the plane so soon—well, it’s a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:14.320
odd way to conceptualize people and what their&nbsp;
financial decisions mean. And just to reiterate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:19.200
I do like one-off sets, and I genuinely understand&nbsp;
the benefits that Wizards of the Coast sees in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:24.000
them. They allow for greater flexibility, they&nbsp;
allow for a higher density of different settings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:29.760
and apparently they sell a bit better too which is&nbsp;
a genuinely understandable consideration. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:36.160
there can be other considerations, because doesn’t&nbsp;
the all one-offs status quo have a cost, too?

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.720
I mentioned earlier that I don’t mind having a&nbsp;
hat set here or there—it’s fine to take a fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:45.600
little break from longer, more serious story&nbsp;
arcs. But that becomes a harder sell when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:51.120
every set is a hat set, or at least when every&nbsp;
set receives the same treatment as a hat set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:56.080
When every set is a one off—when OTJ is treated&nbsp;
the same as Bloomburrow which is treated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.080 --> 00:17:01.040
same as Spiderman which is treated the same as&nbsp;
Lorwyn—it feels a little wrong. It feels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:05.120
the people making the game have no faith in the&nbsp;
worlds they’re creating, even when they clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:10.720
have the ability to create something great.&nbsp;
And, at least for me, this disjointed mishmash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.760
of one-off sets makes me less interested in the&nbsp;
game, and the clear lack of creative conviction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:20.480
that this process exudes makes me less interested&nbsp;
in what Wizards of the Coast has to offer as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:26.080
company that, on some level, produces art. I&nbsp;
wish we could see them being a bit more daring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:31.440
taking some more risks with what they’re putting&nbsp;
out in pursuit of excellence. You could argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:37.040
releasing a neverending string of funky one-off&nbsp;
sets is a version of being bold and taking risks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:41.840
but to me that’s the easier road. The harder&nbsp;
version of being bold is actually committing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:45.920
to a vision, sincerely believing in and&nbsp;
caring about a product enough to see it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:51.280
to a satisfying conclusion, and trusting in your&nbsp;
own ability to tell a multiple-part story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:57.360
people care about. And I don’t know, in 2026,&nbsp;
I think we could use a little more of that.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:02.080
Thanks for watching, and wow this video ended up&nbsp;
going a little deeper than I intended. “Why don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:06.400
I make a fun little video about blocks,” I said to&nbsp;
myself in early March. Let me know what you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:09.840
of what I’m saying here, this is definitely a&nbsp;
bit outside the realm of what I normally produce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

